WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.160
- We're about to watch
"Our Little Family,"

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.780
which follows the everyday lives

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.120
of the Hamill family, who
happen to be little people,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.210
and I'm joined today by the
lovely Pamela Rae Schuller.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:12.540
- I'm a comedian, keynote
speaker, and disability advocate.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.830
- Huge thank you to Google

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.360
for sponsoring a portion of this video.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:16.193
Let's get started.

00:00:16.193 --> 00:00:17.610
Be woop.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.290
- [Michelle] We are a family of five,

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:20.730
and what makes us so special

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.327
is that we are all small.

00:00:22.327 --> 00:00:24.210
We're raising our kids

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.433
to know life can be just as
good small as it is tall.

00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:28.890
- I remember the last
time I lost my tooth.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:29.850
Do you?

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:30.683
- No

00:00:30.683 --> 00:00:32.160
- I put it under the pillow.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:32.993
- For the tooth fairy.

00:00:32.993 --> 00:00:34.499
- And then my dad's like, "I'm
not giving you any money."

00:00:34.499 --> 00:00:35.332
(Pamela laughing)

00:00:35.332 --> 00:00:37.200
And I said, "What about
the tooth fairy, though"?

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.566
He said, "We're Russian
immigrants, no money."

00:00:39.566 --> 00:00:41.070
- [All] We're the Hamills!

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.033
- What up, Hamills?

00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:43.020
Have you seen this show?

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:43.889
- No.
- Me neither.

00:00:43.889 --> 00:00:44.722
- I'm in it.

00:00:44.722 --> 00:00:45.797
I'm into it, not in it.

00:00:45.797 --> 00:00:47.372
- Oh, I was gonna say, you're in it?

00:00:47.372 --> 00:00:50.940
- We have twin daughters, and
they are about to turn three.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.440
Their names are CeCe and Kate,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.110
and they are the complete
opposite of Jack.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.492
They are very high maintenance.

00:00:56.492 --> 00:00:57.325
- Yes.

00:00:57.325 --> 00:00:58.158
- That's like you.

00:00:58.158 --> 00:00:58.991
- I agree fully,

00:00:58.991 --> 00:01:01.440
which is why the twins are
my favorite people already.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.090
- [Michelle] What's
unique about our family

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.380
is that we are all small

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:07.140
and that we all have the
most common form of dwarfism,

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.280
which is achondroplasia.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.840
- Achondroplasia, you heard of it?

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:10.673
- Yes.

00:01:10.673 --> 00:01:12.419
- [Man] Achondroplasia
is when you have a defect

00:01:12.419 --> 00:01:15.240
in changing cartilage into bone

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.320
because when we're born,

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.760
we actually have a lot of cartilage

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.190
that needs to ossify AKA turn into bone.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:21.330
Those who have achondroplasia

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.078
have normal size torsos usually

00:01:23.078 --> 00:01:27.480
and then have shorter than
normal limbs, upper and lower.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.313
- That makes sense.

00:01:28.313 --> 00:01:30.360
So I'm four foot six and a half.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:31.588
So I'm of short stature,

00:01:31.588 --> 00:01:34.830
but I don't have a specific
diagnosis like achondroplasia.

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:36.441
I really think I just forgot to grow.

00:01:36.441 --> 00:01:37.650
- Okay, well, that happens.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:38.483
- I'm little.

00:01:38.483 --> 00:01:39.316
My kids are little.

00:01:39.316 --> 00:01:40.313
I'm very proud to be a little person.

00:01:40.313 --> 00:01:41.540
We're all little together.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.250
- I think there's like
a lot of these views

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:45.900
that people with
disabilities and differences

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.170
are like sitting home sad and
wishing our differences away.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:50.730
In addition to being
four foot six and a half,

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:51.630
I have Tourette syndrome,

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:52.830
and I think for a lot of us,

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:55.710
we've embraced the heck
out of what makes us us.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:59.280
And personally, I don't
wish to not be different,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.680
but I do sometimes wish
that the world was created

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.581
a little bit more with disabilities
and differences in mind.

00:02:04.581 --> 00:02:05.910
(kids yelling)

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.140
- [Michelle] Jack just
started kindergarten,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.420
and he's riding the bus
with all the other kids

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.460
because we don't want him
to feel any different.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:12.390
- Is there any danger to that?

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.620
I don't see any dangers.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:14.453
- No.

00:02:14.453 --> 00:02:15.286
- You know, I will say though,

00:02:15.286 --> 00:02:16.560
the development of children

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.270
who are born with achondroplasia,

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.340
there's an issue with where the brain stem

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:21.990
turns into the spinal cord,

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.390
it's called the cervicomedullary junction,

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:25.770
can sometimes be more narrow.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.030
So when you pick up a child

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.010
that has achondroplasia diagnosis,

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:29.843
you need to be careful

00:02:29.843 --> 00:02:31.186
and really support the back of their head.

00:02:31.186 --> 00:02:32.019
- Oh.

00:02:32.019 --> 00:02:33.600
- So that it doesn't go back
'cause they can have injury.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.914
- No one should let me hold kids without,

00:02:35.914 --> 00:02:37.590
like just at all.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:38.720
- When two little people have kids,

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.140
they have a 50% chance that
the baby will be little.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:44.730
Then we have a 25% chance

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.680
that the baby will be average size.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.540
Then we have a 25% chance

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.449
that the baby will
inherit the dwarfism gene

00:02:50.449 --> 00:02:52.050
from both of us.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.870
And if the baby inherits
that gene from both of us,

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:56.349
they call it a double dominant baby,

00:02:56.349 --> 00:02:58.829
and after it's born, it
doesn't live very long.

00:02:58.829 --> 00:03:00.270
- In medical textbooks,

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:03.870
it usually says someone who is
homozygous for this condition

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:05.504
is incompatible with life.

00:03:05.504 --> 00:03:08.220
I would say there's a
possibility that they can be born

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.330
and sustain life early on,

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.340
but then they usually end up passing.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.880
- Same day that I found out
that I was pregnant with twins,

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.938
I also found out that I
had a medical complication,

00:03:16.938 --> 00:03:20.040
that there was a blood clot in
the middle of the two babies.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.230
- Blood clot in the
middle of the two babies,

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:23.820
I'm guessing in the umbilical vein.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:24.810
That's really rare.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.776
- They had told me that if
that blood clot were to break

00:03:27.776 --> 00:03:29.700
within my first trimester

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.760
I could lose either one
or both of those babies.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.010
- In general, pregnancy
creates a higher risk

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:36.060
for having blood clots.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:37.200
That's a known risk.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.033
- Okay.

00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:40.712
- But having twins elevates
that risk even more,

00:03:40.712 --> 00:03:43.590
specifically later in the pregnancy

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.740
- Luckily, we were blessed
that the babies got bigger

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.180
and the blood clot got smaller.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.013
- Oh yeah.

00:03:49.013 --> 00:03:51.360
- But then we had to
test to see if they had

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:52.620
the double dominant gene.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.747
First, we found out that
CeCe was gonna be little.

00:03:55.747 --> 00:03:57.117
- And a girl.

00:03:57.117 --> 00:03:58.376
- Just wait.

00:03:58.376 --> 00:03:59.970
That's the most important.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.820
And then we knew that she was healthy,

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.170
and it was gonna be a girl.

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:08.940
- Do you think about or care
about, if you have a child,

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:10.050
what their height is gonna be?

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:11.962
- No, I'd be more afraid
that they'd be athletic.

00:04:11.962 --> 00:04:16.950
Like, I can raise a kid of any height.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:18.960
I can raise a kid with Tourettes,

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.600
but like what am I gonna
go to a basketball game?

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:22.620
- Well, you can watch.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:23.940
- It's really common for little people

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.230
to have a fear of dogs.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.024
- I don't like that dog.

00:04:27.024 --> 00:04:28.371
(both laughing)

00:04:28.371 --> 00:04:30.390
- I wonder how they'd feel about Bear?

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:31.410
Bear.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:32.370
- We're best friends.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:33.608
- Hi.

00:04:33.608 --> 00:04:34.441
High five.

00:04:34.441 --> 00:04:36.291
- Oh, he has to get the whole body up.

00:04:36.291 --> 00:04:37.350
- Oh, he has to, oh.

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:38.471
- Can I get one too, bud?

00:04:38.471 --> 00:04:39.750
Oh, thank you.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:40.583
- Can I get one?

00:04:40.583 --> 00:04:41.416
- Thank you, buddy.

00:04:41.416 --> 00:04:43.950
- Can I get one, one more high five?

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:44.783
- He's like, dad, I gave 10 already.

00:04:44.783 --> 00:04:46.500
- Please, just one more high five.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:47.398
- My turn.

00:04:47.398 --> 00:04:48.231
Oh!

00:04:48.231 --> 00:04:49.310
- Oh, you love her more!

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:50.143
You're a traitor.

00:04:50.143 --> 00:04:51.484
- You're my best boy.

00:04:51.484 --> 00:04:52.830
- You're a traitor.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.020
- Average size people
really don't think about it,

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.620
but imagine if dogs
were the size of bears,

00:04:58.620 --> 00:04:59.760
and they were coming up to you.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.270
- Did she say size of bears?

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:03.090
Back to the show in just a second,

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:04.680
but first I want to thank Google

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:06.750
for sponsoring this portion of the video.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:10.470
Accessibility is incredibly
important to all humans,

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:13.440
as we've seen on the show, as
I've seen with my patients,

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.301
and as Pam has seen in her everyday life.

00:05:15.301 --> 00:05:17.370
Pam, why is accessibility so important?

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:19.920
- Well, as someone with a
neurological movement disorder

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:21.210
and of short stature,

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.603
accessibility is my access to the world.

00:05:23.603 --> 00:05:25.110
When I can access the world

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:27.660
I can bring my best self to my community.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.970
- I was actually really hyped
when Google approached me

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:30.930
with this partnership

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.238
because they take accessibility seriously.

00:05:33.238 --> 00:05:35.460
On all Android devices,

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:37.560
Google actually has these features

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.480
that I think are revolutionary.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:40.770
The one that interests me the most

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:42.390
is the live transcribe one,

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:43.620
where if you're having a conversation

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:45.630
with someone who's deaf
or hard of hearing,

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:48.053
it actually creates live
captioning that's really accurate.

00:05:48.053 --> 00:05:50.670
- I'm obsessed with Voice Access.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:52.050
I think a lot of times

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.820
the media likes you to
think that Tourettes

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.290
is just like yelling words,

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:58.200
but for me, it primarily
manifests through movements,

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.750
and for me, I have almost
no control over my hands.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:01.583
That's what I use.

00:06:01.583 --> 00:06:02.580
I speak into my phone,

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.320
and it's able to capture what I'm saying.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.770
- And the one that I think
Pam and I both really enjoy,

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:09.361
and we probably use like, regularly,

00:06:09.361 --> 00:06:12.110
is the accessible places feature,

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:14.136
which, when you search on Google maps,

00:06:14.136 --> 00:06:15.720
you actually get information

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.120
about how accessible a
certain location could be.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:19.221
- These features are incredible.

00:06:19.221 --> 00:06:22.500
- Yeah, I mean, we wanna
have a more inclusive world.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:23.640
It's happening right in front of our eyes

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:24.720
with this technology.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.070
Again, huge thank you to Google

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:27.780
for sponsoring this portion of the video.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:29.100
Let's get back to our show.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.410
- Does he carry a stool?

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:34.839
Because you used to have a
stool from first grade through-

00:06:34.839 --> 00:06:36.000
- [Michelle] Yeah.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.110
- I mean, eighth grade.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:39.420
- I live in a studio
apartment in New York City,

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.340
and I have six stools in one apartment.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:42.173
- Really?

00:06:42.173 --> 00:06:43.006
- I can't reach my microwave

00:06:43.006 --> 00:06:43.860
because in New York, they've put them

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.690
above your oven, stove,
whatever that thing is.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.137
In my closet, I can't reach 80% of it.

00:06:48.137 --> 00:06:49.260
I have a stool there.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:50.220
I have one in my bathroom

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.290
because most of the
storage is above the sink.

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:53.220
In my living room,

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:55.530
I've got one because my
bed is a normal size bed,

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:56.760
and I have to run and jump into it,

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.860
and sometimes you don't
wanna be an acrobat.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.960
- [Michelle] Today, we are
meeting Jack's teacher,

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:02.730
and to be honest, I'm not exactly sure

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:03.660
what she's going to say.

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:05.730
Does he ever like sit there
and go, "Oh, Mrs. Clayborn,

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:06.563
I can't see?"

00:07:06.563 --> 00:07:08.940
- He told the boys and girls,
"You guys need to move over

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:10.620
'cause I cannot even do
the Rectangle Rumba."

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:12.720
So he moved himself to the front

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:14.098
and stood right in front of the-

00:07:14.098 --> 00:07:14.931
- Okay, good.

00:07:14.931 --> 00:07:15.764
- Good self-advocacy,

00:07:15.764 --> 00:07:16.597
- [Pamela] Yeah, I love that.

00:07:16.597 --> 00:07:18.540
I think that's something we
should be working with all kids,

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:20.340
not just kids with
disabilities and differences.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.470
Like, how do you speak up

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.990
if you're not getting what
you need to be successful?

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:26.700
I talk a lot about disability inclusion,

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:28.590
but in reality, we all
need different things

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:29.729
at different times to be successful.

00:07:29.729 --> 00:07:31.290
- You have to be this high?

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:33.660
- You can go to the left, Jack, I think.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:35.676
And then I think the girls
need to go to the right.

00:07:35.676 --> 00:07:36.977
- [Dan] It's the same thing.

00:07:36.977 --> 00:07:37.810
It goes to the same place.

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:38.643
- [Michelle] At the Bouncy Pillow,

00:07:38.643 --> 00:07:39.660
we had to make a decision.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:42.301
Do we send Jack with people his own age,

00:07:42.301 --> 00:07:45.838
or do we send him with
the people his own size?

00:07:45.838 --> 00:07:47.057
- It's saying by height.

00:07:47.057 --> 00:07:48.330
- Yes.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:51.030
- So why are we trying to
create a new interpretation?

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:52.980
- Because also, he might
wanna play with his friends.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:53.813
- [Man] Fair.

00:07:53.813 --> 00:07:54.810
The reason I worry about this,

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:56.370
and the reason I'm being strict about it

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:58.290
is because if you're like
in an amusement park,

00:07:58.290 --> 00:07:59.883
and there's these signs about height-

00:07:59.883 --> 00:08:00.716
- [Pamela] Yeah.

00:08:00.716 --> 00:08:01.560
- [Man] It's because
of safety and security

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:03.181
of the seat belts and locking mechanisms.

00:08:03.181 --> 00:08:04.014
- [Pamela] I get it.

00:08:04.014 --> 00:08:04.847
I can't ride half those rides.

00:08:04.847 --> 00:08:05.680
- Really?

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:06.513
- I'm too short to ride half the rides.

00:08:06.513 --> 00:08:08.253
- I can't either because of my fear.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:11.820
Why are you laughing at me?

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:12.653
- Sorry.

00:08:12.653 --> 00:08:13.665
- That's not fair

00:08:13.665 --> 00:08:15.140
- Because you made it sound like

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:16.740
you cant up for like an accessible reason.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:17.573
- That is an accessible reason.

00:08:17.573 --> 00:08:18.912
- No, it's a mental health reason.

00:08:18.912 --> 00:08:19.803
- [Man] That's accessible.

00:08:19.803 --> 00:08:20.636
- Okay, fine.

00:08:20.636 --> 00:08:22.512
- Can we have some cookies?

00:08:22.512 --> 00:08:24.175
Thank you so much.

00:08:24.175 --> 00:08:26.200
I'm living in an average size world.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:28.410
It's not gonna adapt to me.

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:30.870
I have to figure out how
to adapt to the world.

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:31.703
How about these?

00:08:31.703 --> 00:08:32.970
Look.

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:33.803
Come here.

00:08:33.803 --> 00:08:34.636
Why don't we do these?

00:08:34.636 --> 00:08:36.510
- My grocery store in New York City

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:37.980
refers to me as the climber.

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:39.930
At one point, they were like,
"You have to stop climbing."

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:41.772
And I was like, "Okay, then you either

00:08:41.772 --> 00:08:44.220
need to have step stools
that are available to me

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:45.960
or have someone who is available

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:46.860
to reach the things down,"

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:47.895
because in New York we build up.

00:08:47.895 --> 00:08:48.728
- Yeah.

00:08:48.728 --> 00:08:52.304
- [Pamela] And so I can't
reach 70% of the things I need

00:08:52.304 --> 00:08:53.308
at a grocery store.

00:08:53.308 --> 00:08:55.110
- So what did they say to you?

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:56.730
- They told me they would
have someone help me,

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:57.563
and I still climb.

00:08:57.563 --> 00:08:58.396
- Oh.

00:08:58.396 --> 00:09:00.203
- Why are you so small
and the other one's big?

00:09:00.203 --> 00:09:01.913
- Because I was just born small.

00:09:01.913 --> 00:09:04.200
- But why are you the same size?

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:06.210
- [Michelle] Because we
were both born small.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:07.170
- I love that about children,

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:08.190
that they're not afraid.

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:09.270
They're just being genuine.

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:10.103
- Yes.

00:09:10.103 --> 00:09:11.430
- I think that's the most authentic

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:12.330
and genuine way to go about it.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:13.163
- I agree.

00:09:13.163 --> 00:09:13.996
Kids have questions

00:09:13.996 --> 00:09:15.390
because they're learning about the world,

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:17.490
and if we shush a kid
when they have questions,

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:19.427
we're teaching them to feel shame

00:09:19.427 --> 00:09:21.420
in the presence of disability.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:23.550
So while not everyone is
comfortable explaining to a kid

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:26.460
maybe why they're little
or why they twitch

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:29.400
or why they use a wheelchair,
for people who are,

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.190
it's great 'cause we're
giving kids information,

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:35.141
and kids I think are
inclusive when they get it.

00:09:35.141 --> 00:09:38.937
- Whenever I'm more honest
about what I don't know

00:09:38.937 --> 00:09:42.857
and what I can learn, the
more engagement I get.

00:09:42.857 --> 00:09:43.890
- God, I wish more

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:45.090
doctors did that.
- Well, it's scary.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:46.320
- I wish more doctors would be like,

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:47.790
I don't actually know
a lot about Tourettes.

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:48.930
Can you tell me, Pam?

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:50.280
If you put me in a room with 10,000 people

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:51.330
who are four foot six and a half

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:52.770
and have a whole lot of Tourette syndrome,

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:55.020
every single one of us would
have a totally different story.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:56.482
- [Dan] It'd be cool to build
one sometime in our yard,

00:09:56.482 --> 00:09:58.110
something that would be-

00:09:58.110 --> 00:09:58.943
- A play set?

00:09:58.943 --> 00:10:00.000
- [Dan] On totally equal grounds.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.060
Like, just design it so he
doesn't know it's any different,

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:06.472
but he could climb up as fast
as the other kids or whatever.

00:10:06.472 --> 00:10:07.305
You know what I mean?

00:10:07.305 --> 00:10:08.490
- You mean for you to build it?

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:09.426
- Yeah.

00:10:09.426 --> 00:10:10.860
(Michelle laughing)

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:12.030
- Wow.

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:13.440
Disrespectful laugh.

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.210
- I love that like, yeah,

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:18.121
this show's about a
family of little people,

00:10:18.121 --> 00:10:20.317
but it's also just about like a family.

00:10:20.317 --> 00:10:21.240
- Yeah, exactly.

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:22.073
- And they are-

00:10:22.073 --> 00:10:22.906
- They talk smack to each other.

00:10:22.906 --> 00:10:23.739
- That was like a sick burn.

00:10:23.739 --> 00:10:25.110
(saw screeching)

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:26.850
- [Dan] The challenge of
building this play set

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:27.840
is I have to make things

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:29.637
spaced a little bit
closer together for Jack,

00:10:29.637 --> 00:10:31.378
but I also wanna space it in a way

00:10:31.378 --> 00:10:33.574
that every other kid in
the world can use it too,

00:10:33.574 --> 00:10:35.370
and it's not exclusively for him.

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:36.760
- Great example of universal design.

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:40.080
Universal design is this idea
that, as we create anything,

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:43.910
a building a community, a
structure, a phone, a tool,

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:47.880
we create it with the goal
of as many people as possible

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:50.136
being able to use it at the
fullest extent possible.

00:10:50.136 --> 00:10:51.090
- Oh, okay.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:51.923
- So really-

00:10:51.923 --> 00:10:53.070
- Give me an example
of that, besides this.

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:55.920
- When you go to open a
door and there's a button,

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:57.480
and the door opens automatically,

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:59.730
that was initially designed
for people who use wheelchairs,

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:02.160
but think about someone
carrying groceries.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:03.750
Think about someone carrying kids.

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:05.070
Think about someone who maybe

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:06.810
doesn't have use of their hands or arms.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:08.610
You do something for one or two,

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:10.853
and it's often beneficial
for so many more.

00:11:10.853 --> 00:11:13.680
- [Michelle] I was born
and raised in Annapolis.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:15.180
I have never really left.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:18.450
I stay here because no one sees me

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:19.462
as a little person anymore.

00:11:19.462 --> 00:11:20.880
- Michelle.
- Hmm.

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:23.156
- Describe them hmm I just heard.

00:11:23.156 --> 00:11:25.412
- It's just an interesting way to...

00:11:25.412 --> 00:11:27.660
I think, I'm trying to figure out

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:29.217
like the best way to say this.

00:11:29.217 --> 00:11:31.800
I think it's okay to see disability.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:33.060
She is of short stature.

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:34.290
She's a little person.

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:35.970
I think what she probably means,

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:36.930
or I'm never gonna put

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:38.040
words in her mouth.
- People don't judge her.

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:39.300
- Don't treat her differently.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:40.133
- Yeah.

00:11:40.133 --> 00:11:41.040
- That's what I think probably.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:41.873
Like I think-
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:11:41.873 --> 00:11:42.706
I don't wanna-

00:11:42.706 --> 00:11:43.539
- Of course, I think.

00:11:43.539 --> 00:11:44.520
- I actually don't love
it when someone is like,

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:47.324
when I'm with you, Pam, I
don't see Tourettes anymore.

00:11:47.324 --> 00:11:49.710
I bark, like, yes you do.

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:51.960
- I take advantage of
that when I'm with you.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:53.040
- [Instructor] Push!

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:53.873
Yes!

00:11:53.873 --> 00:11:55.650
- Some average size people
think that little people

00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:56.807
can't work out, and that's a total myth.

00:11:56.807 --> 00:12:00.060
- Dude, you work out harder than me,

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:02.070
except when I'm boxing,
training for professional fight,

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:02.940
I'm doing a lot more.

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:05.428
But otherwise, your baseline
of working out is insane.

00:12:05.428 --> 00:12:06.480
- Thank you.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:07.530
I love working out,

00:12:07.530 --> 00:12:10.050
and the gym is made for
men of average height.

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:11.850
The elliptical, I was
told I'm too short to use.

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:13.770
I'm too short to use any of the bikes.

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:15.180
The things that you pull down,

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:16.560
I can't even reach it.
- Lat pull downs, yeah.

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:17.432
- As a little person, I'm really lucky

00:12:17.432 --> 00:12:20.105
that I don't have any
physical complications

00:12:20.105 --> 00:12:22.645
that cause me not to be able to do

00:12:22.645 --> 00:12:25.980
what I physically like, want to do.

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:27.300
- Those with achondroplasia

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:28.716
have higher rates of osteoarthritis,

00:12:28.716 --> 00:12:31.290
so they can have a lot of joint pain,

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:32.310
and it could be very painful.

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:34.122
Also, those with
achondroplasia, especially kids,

00:12:34.122 --> 00:12:37.918
have higher rates of frequent
middle ear infections,

00:12:37.918 --> 00:12:40.472
early development of sleep apnea.

00:12:40.472 --> 00:12:41.640
- Huh?

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:42.990
- So the there's things you have to,

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:45.870
what we call in medicine is
give anticipatory guidance.

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:46.703
- That's interesting.

00:12:46.703 --> 00:12:47.760
I've had a ton of ear infections,

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:48.690
and no one ever told me

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:50.474
it could be connected to something else.

00:12:50.474 --> 00:12:52.357
- See, if I was your doctor,
and you were my patient.

00:12:52.357 --> 00:12:53.340
- Okay.

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:54.621
- I'll just cut yours, okay?

00:12:54.621 --> 00:12:56.660
- [CeCe] No, I want a knife.

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:58.650
(Pamela laughing)

00:12:58.650 --> 00:12:59.529
- He's going for the cheese.

00:12:59.529 --> 00:13:00.362
- He eats how I eat the cheese.

00:13:00.362 --> 00:13:01.195
- A man of my heart.

00:13:01.195 --> 00:13:02.028
- I do extra cheese.

00:13:02.028 --> 00:13:03.120
- We're gonna start eating healthier.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:05.460
Maybe tomorrow, since what
you need pizza tonight.

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:07.440
- The story of my life, maybe tomorrow.

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:09.840
- I really want the kids to
have better eating habits

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:12.570
because as little people,
if you're overweight,

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:14.430
it's more pressure on your knees,

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:15.720
your joints, and your back.

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:17.940
- You know what point we never made clear

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:19.020
medically about this?

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:20.340
If you're born with achondroplasia,

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:21.540
there's no other medical conditions

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:22.860
that you're diagnosed with,

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:24.480
you still have a normal life expectancy.

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:25.680
- Oh, that's good to know.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:27.300
- Michelle's taking a cooking class.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:29.430
The girls are taking dance.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:30.450
Made me kind of realize

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:32.670
that there's something I
wanted to do for a long time.

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:34.636
- What do you guess that he's gonna do?

00:13:34.636 --> 00:13:37.980
- What I hope it is is like a magic class.

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:39.496
- I was looking up skydiving.

00:13:39.496 --> 00:13:40.377
- Oh.

00:13:40.377 --> 00:13:41.760
- And I kind of signed myself up.

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:42.593
- My god.

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:45.360
- You gonna go skydiving?

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:46.193
- There we go.

00:13:46.193 --> 00:13:47.279
Oh, oh.

00:13:47.279 --> 00:13:48.570
- Right there.

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:50.070
See, look, his parachute opened!

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:51.681
I'm so relieved Dan's parachute opened.

00:13:51.681 --> 00:13:53.310
That was my biggest-

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:56.059
- I guess that is a thing that you wanted.

00:13:56.059 --> 00:13:57.219
- [Josh] There's your little boy.

00:13:57.219 --> 00:13:58.052
You see him?

00:13:58.052 --> 00:13:59.937
- [Dan] Yeah, hey, Jack.

00:13:59.937 --> 00:14:01.299
- My dad can do anything!

00:14:01.299 --> 00:14:02.420
- Yeah.

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:03.470
- And so can I.

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:07.080
- Do you know that Pam roasts
me literally all the time?

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:10.260
She actually roasted my entire
Instagram picture catalog.

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:11.430
Click here to check that out.

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:13.771
As always, stay happy and healthy and-

00:14:13.771 --> 00:14:15.360
- Fabulous.

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:16.193
- You said nuggety last time.

